INT. HOSPITAL ROOFTOP GARDEN - MORNING

A walled terrace above the oncology wing. Raised beds of lavender and rosemary. Wrought-iron benches, a trellis of late roses. Pigeons strut. The city hums far below.

DR. IMELDA FAIRWEATHER-NKEMELU, 50s, in a white coat gone limp, sits on a bench with a paper cup. She has not slept. A gray streak runs through her hair.

MR. ARTHUR PENHALIGON, 70s, in a hospital gown and a corduroy cardigan, sits beside her, IV pole parked at his knee. His cheeks are hollow. His eyes are very clear.

His daughter, JULIETTE PENHALIGON, 40s, in a trench coat, perches on the bench's arm, hands clasped.

A nurse, NURSE BEATRIX OYELAKIN, 30s, in blue scrubs, hovers at the door with a clipboard.

PENHALIGON
You'll tell it plainly, Doctor.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
Yes.

PENHALIGON
Good. Everyone else has been very gentle.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
The scan shows the tumor has spread to the liver.

PENHALIGON
How long?

DR. FAIRWEATHER
Weeks. Perhaps a few months.

JULIETTE
(quietly)
Dad.

PENHALIGON
It's all right, Juliette.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
We can keep you comfortable. We can plan the time.

PENHALIGON
Plan.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
Yes.

PENHALIGON
I have been planning since I was a young man. I planned to write a novel. I planned to see Lisbon.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
Have you seen Lisbon?

PENHALIGON
Never.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
Then there is something to plan.

PENHALIGON
(a thin smile)
You are a surprising doctor.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
I have been told.

JULIETTE
(to the doctor)
Can he travel?

DR. FAIRWEATHER
With supervision. In two weeks. Not more.

BEATRIX
(from the door)
Doctor, the families are waiting in Bay Three.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
(rising, to Beatrix)
Two minutes.

PENHALIGON
Doctor.

DR. FAIRWEATHER
Yes, Mr. Penhaligon.

PENHALIGON
Thank you for sitting.

She nods. Touches his shoulder for a moment, light as a petal, and goes. The door closes. A pigeon lands on the trellis. Juliette takes her father's hand. He looks at the lavender.
